  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Subtropical Storm Josephine located well off \nthe Mid-Atlantic coast will move even farther away today, with \ncoastal impacts and hazardous marine conditions gradually \ndwindling.\n\nSubtropical Storm Josephine located well off the Mid-Atlantic \ncoast will gradually turns more to the northeast today. \nMeanwhile, strong surface high pressure is re-centering across \nthe Canadian Maritimes. This is allowing the pressure gradient\nto begin relaxing. \n\nImpacts will continue to dwindle for our coastal and marine \nareas. The multi-day north-northeast wind has resulted in 10+ \nfoot waves just offshore. These big waves breaking onto the \nbeaches will result in some additional beach erosion especially \nduring times of high tide. Peak wind gusts should be notably\nlower today, more like 20-30 mph even at the coast. Any\nlingering mid and high clouds should dissipate. \n\nAs Subtropical Storm Josephine moves farther out to sea, an \nincoming system from the northwest will push across the region,\nbut no precipitation and few clouds are expected with it... its\nmain function is to move Josephine away. This will further reduce\nthe marine and coastal impacts, while providing a fresh shot of\nchilly air. There will still be a notable north- northwest \nbreeze today as the pressure gradient tightens again as another \nstrong surface high moves towards us from Canada. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Elevated tide cycles persist due to Subtropical \nStorm Josephine well of the coast resulting in widespread minor\ncoastal flooding continuing in Ocean County, NJ and Sussex\nCounty, DE.\n\nAs Subtropical Storm Josephine lingers well off the coast elevated \ntide cycles are persisting some areas. \n\nIn Ocean County, New Jersey and Sussex County, Delaware, water \nlevels remain elevated and minor tidal flooding is continuing, \nparticularly in the back bays. Another round of minor coastal \nflooding is looking more likely this evening, so the coastal\nflood advisory was extended through this evening. Elsewhere,\nonly spotty minor flooding is expected during high tide this\nmorning and this evening.\n\nAt this time we are not forecasting any coastal flooding for the \nMaryland eastern shore. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Frost impacts possible Friday night and Saturday \nnight where the growing season continues. \n\nAs an upper-level trough pivots across the Northeast on Friday,\na potentially strong mid level ridge in the Plains to Midwest \nshifts eastward some into the weekend. This will support strong \nsurface high pressure initially centered near the Great Lakes and \nportions of eastern Canada, which then shifts eastward into the \nNortheast and Mid-Atlantic Friday and over the weekend.\n\nA chilly and dry air mass will accompany this strong surface \nhigh, and as the high itself settles more overhead the winds \nwill decouple. This will set the stage for efficient radiational\ncooling both Friday night and Saturday night. The forecast lows\nare in the 30s for much of the area (away from the coasts and \noutside of the urban centers) with a frost potential. It is \npossible that if the radiational cooling ends up being stronger,\nthen some areas could get to freezing or a little below. \nOverall though, frost impacts are possible where the growing \nseason remains active.",
